WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — agency (90s) Narrator Joy

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.102
First, an agent must aim at a goal,

2
00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:07.157
act on the world, and stay within an energy budget.

3
00:00:07.657 --> 00:00:10.807
Second, physics prices every decision,

4
00:00:10.807 --> 00:00:17.273
creating one optimal pace between slow idle drain and frantic switching waste.

5
00:00:17.773 --> 00:00:25.106
Third, among systems that finish a task correctly, the smarter one is whichever spent less energy.

6
00:00:25.606 --> 00:00:30.158
Picture driving across a wide desert on a strict fuel grant. Idling

7
00:00:30.158 --> 00:00:35.456
in traffic burns baseline power without moving you forward, while sprinting at

8
00:00:35.456 --> 00:00:41.027
top speed multiplies switching heat until your tank runs dry before reaching town.

9
00:00:41.527 --> 00:00:46.841
To qualify as an agent, a system locks in a testable prediction before acting,

10
00:00:46.841 --> 00:00:52.905
changes the outside world rather than editing its own sensors, updates its internal state

11
00:00:52.905 --> 00:00:59.174
from the outcome, and writes a clean refusal receipt whenever the energy budget falls short.

12
00:00:59.674 --> 00:01:05.402
Across more than twenty thousand puzzle episodes on two laptops, an unguided random

13
00:01:05.402 --> 00:01:10.508
baseline looked great on raw bits per joule only because its fast failures

14
00:01:10.508 --> 00:01:17.134
were cheap, whereas gating by full task completion ranked our goal-holding selectors accurately.

15
00:01:17.634 --> 00:01:24.469
Stop mistaking brute energy spend for intelligence. Demand a verified correct answer first,

16
00:01:24.469 --> 00:01:33.784
enforce hard energy budgets with transparent refusal receipts, and rank every passing system strictly by total joules spent.
